# This file is vendored. Do not edit it.

`model/`, `model-en/` and `training/base-model/` come from
[`owensong/Inflect-Micro-v2`](https://huggingface.co/owensong/Inflect-Micro-v2),
not from us. `model/` is the Azerbaijani adaptation's full export package;
`training/base-model/` is the complete English package, untracked; `model-en/`
is the English weights alone, tracked through Git LFS so a clone speaks both
languages.

- **`model/checksums.sha256` verifies 24 files in that directory.** Any edit
  breaks verification, and the check is part of the release process.
- **`model-en/checksums.sha256` verifies its six vendored files.** `README.md`
  there is ours and is deliberately outside the checksum list.
- `.gitattributes` marks `model/**` and `model-en/**` as `linguist-vendored` so
  they stay out of our language statistics and collapsed in diffs.
- `training/base-model/` is not tracked at all — it is the complete 38 MB
  package fetched by `python training/scripts/download_model.py`, needed only
  for training work. Editing it edits a download.

## The characters here are IPA, not Azerbaijani

`model/runtime/text/symbols.py` and the phoneme code contain `ə`, `ɣ`, `ʃ`, `ɫ`
and friends. These are IPA phoneme symbols. A search for Azerbaijani letters
matches them. Do not translate, transliterate or normalise anything here.

## Never import the English package's Python

`model-en/` deliberately holds only `model.pth` and `config.json` (plus the
licence, notices and citation that must travel with them). The upstream package
also ships `inference.py`, `models.py` and `text/` — modules with the *same
names* as the ones in `model/`. Importing both in one process returns whichever
was imported first, silently, with no error to notice.

`aztts/en_voice.py` therefore loads the English weights into `model/`'s runtime
and phonemises English itself with eSpeak's `en-us` voice, handing the result
over as `phonemes=`. Keep it that way.

## If the model needs to behave differently

Wrap it. `aztts/engine.py` exists precisely for this: it loads the package,
then adds censoring (`az_profanity.py`), Azerbaijani normalisation (`az_text.py`),
chunking (`az_chunk.py`) and optional restressing (`az_prosody.py`) around it. Every gap between "what the
upstream package does" and "what Azerbaijani needs" is closed in `aztts/`.

Two upstream facts worth knowing before you try to patch anything:

- `model/config.json` declares `accepts_prephonemized_input: true`, so phonemes
  can come from any frontend — you do not have to go through eSpeak.
- The package splits text itself only after 280 characters, which is far too
  late for this model. `az_chunk.py` cuts at ~15 words instead.
